Z||000000||fiero87||08-01-2000||10:19 AM||||Hello,<P>I have been working on refinishing my dash in  my 87 SC.  I'v removed all the panels i want and colored them white, car color.  I also have a set of white face gauge overlays that i want to install.  Only problem is that during my attempt to remove the gauges i can't seem to get the padded cluster cover to come off the rest of the gauge pod?  Anyone else ever taken this thing apart?  Absolutly any info is greatly appreciated.  Thanks<P>Also, I managed to break the plexiglass cover over the gauges in an attempt to remove it.  Anyone got a parts car with this still in tact?  I'd be willing to purchase it...Thanks<BR><P>------------------<BR>Tim<BR>87 Fiero SC <BR>90 Daytona VNT(1 of 596)<BR>94 Duster (Winter ride)||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfixit58||08-01-2000||10:58 AM||||You need to remove the control panel surround (the padded thing around the radio and comfort controls). Then remove the four screws ar the top, lift the gauges and unclip the wiring harness. That's all there is to it.<P>White interior??<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||286SE'S||08-01-2000||12:23 PM||||I don't thik he is tring to remove the Aux gauge pod (oil press+amp) but the main instrument panel. It must be unscrewed from from the bottom and back side. I know you can remove the  whole pod without taking off the steering wheel.<BR>Take off the back cover of the pod and you will see two big bolts at the bottom of the support. There are also two of the same bolts under the cover at the bottom of steering wheel. Disconnect all the wires to the gauge cluster and the headlight switch and trunk release/ defroster switches. Tilt the steering wheel all the way down and lift it out. There are screws holding the cluister to the plastic pod shell. The clear plastic window can only be removed with the cluster out of the pod because the screws are covered when it is installed. Someone on e-bay was selling a pod with a GLASS gauge cover. Anyone ever seen one of these? The OEM plastice seems to fog and scratch easily.<BR>I like the Idea of a white interior!<BR>Terry<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero87||08-02-2000||10:53 AM||||Hey,<BR>Thanks for the info guys.  To clear up any confusion i just took all the panels that are usually covered by dash kits and painted them a high gloss white, with about 5 layers of clear coat.  Did the shift knob too.  If anyone want pictures i'd be happy to post.  Thanks again<BR>Tim<P>------------------<BR>Tim<BR>87 Fiero SC <BR>90 Daytona VNT(1 of 596)<BR>94 Duster (Winter ride)||||reg||10
